Maxima regression checks for the ECL transition
==============================================
Checked 2026-10-11 in Debian unstable arm64, limited to CPUs 0-3.

The regular maxima source package in testing and unstable is
5.49.0+dsfg-6. Its binary uses GCL, so it does not need an ECL ABI
rebuild. Keep its upstream version unchanged for this transition.
maxima-sage was already at 5.50.0-2 in unstable before this work;
the proposed 5.50.0-3 rebuild keeps that upstream version.

The patched ECL discovers /usr/lib/ecl/maxima.fas via (require :maxima).
Debian Sage therefore uses an empty MAXIMA_FAS, and the Debian-specific
Meson pathname fallback is removed. Maxima installs its FAS with dh_install;
ECL creates the default module directory even without Maxima installed.

Reviewed regular Maxima's seven active patches:

  5.29-refresh: viewer and Emacs integration, not shipped by maxima-sage.
  sys-proclaim-fix: GCL build machinery, not the ECL build.
  create_index: CHM index generation, not used for these Debian packages.
  cl-info and cl-info-2: relocated documentation indexes. maxima-sage
    already has its own relocation patch and installs the index and
    uncompressed info files together. describe(integrate,exact) works.
  wna-err-fix: present upstream in 5.50.0. errcatch(nfloat()) gives the
    correct argument error in regular Maxima and both ECL builds.
  draw-take-into-account-gnuplot_file_args: present upstream in 5.50.0.

Packaging was read from the current archive Sources index and verified
against SHA256 b5ba8a234bd2046d0f7bd058a8ce7c53c064cc52a4c24005baecdfa504e0fca2
for maxima_5.49.0+dsfg-6.debian.tar.xz. Commented-out patches in its
series file were not treated as applied downstream fixes.

An upstream Sage compatibility fix was missing from Debian Sage 10.9:
the overridden Maxima principal function used pcprntd instead of
*pcprntd*. With the original wrapper, integrate(1/x^3,x,-1,3,
algorithm='maxima') raises an unbound-variable error; default integration
falls back to another integrator and returns NaN. Backport
u0-maxima-principal-value.patch takes the exact one-line correction from
https://github.com/sagemath/sage/commit/7881362be8336b8e66fee7cce97befcc9a252404

The added focused regression rejects the original wrapper. After the
fix, both default and explicit Maxima integration raise the expected
ValueError, while the convergent integral of 1/(x^2+1) on [-1,1]
still returns pi/2. These checks pass with the archive ECL 24.5/Maxima
5.50.0-2 pair and with the updated ECL 26.5.5/Maxima 5.50.0-3 pair.
All 204 sage.libs.ecl and 227 maxima_lib doctests pass with the update.
The focused autopkgtest also passes, including the restored interrupt.

There is still a genuine upstream-version performance regression:

  integrate(log(1+4/5*sin(x)),x,-3.1415,3.1415)

Regular Debian Maxima 5.49.0 returns the unevaluated integral in about
0.2 seconds. Archive Maxima 5.50.0/ECL 24.5 exceeds 15 seconds; the
updated 5.50.0/ECL 26.5.5 build exceeds 30 seconds standalone and via
Sage. Each reproduction was stopped with a bounded timeout. This is
already reported at https://bugs.debian.org/1150619 and is not introduced
by the ECL rebuild. The report's other timeout involves SymPy and also
occurs with Maxima 5.49.0. This backport does not fix those timeouts.

Other 5.50.0 output changes still need Sage doctest updates. For example,
the Fresnel antiderivative has a different expression, but applying
radcan(exponentialize(diff(F,x)-sin(x^2))) returns zero with both ECL
versions, confirming that its derivative equals the original integrand.

Broader Sage calculus and interface tests were attempted, not declared
passing. The installed archive Sage 10.9-3 also has PARI extensions linked
to libpari-gmp-tls.so.9, while current cypari2 uses .so.10. A fresh process
reproduces its rational-to-PARI conversion crash without loading ECL.
Only Sage's ECL extension was rebuilt for these checks. A complete Sage
rebuild against the current PARI/cypari2 is needed before interpreting
those broader tests, and the integration timeouts remain unresolved.

These are draft source-package changes, not a claim that the complete
Sage testsuite passes or that Maxima 5.50.0 is ready for stable migration.
